 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Hi, the light in my Neff oven has stopped working (model B1ACE4HN0B). Oven works fine otherwise. Can I change the bulb myself and what bulb do I need?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, you can change it yourself and you will need a special heat-resistant 300°C bulb with an SES (small Edison screw) fitting. The most likely cause is simply a blown bulb, though less commonly it could be the lamp holder or wiring. First, make sure the oven is switched off at the mains and completely cool to touch before attempting any work. Then carefully remove the old bulb and check if the new one fits securely into the socket without bent contacts."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Thanks. I'll isolate it at the cooker switch and let the oven cool down first. Is there anything else I should check before I take the glass cover off?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, just make sure you have the correct replacement bulb ready before you start. While it is likely just a blown bulb, if the new one doesn't work, the issue could be with the lamp holder or wiring. Please let me know how the bulb change goes and whether the light comes back on afterwards."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Ok, oven is isolated and cool now. I got the glass cover off using a rubber glove for grip. The old bulb says E14, 25W, 300C and the filament looks broken. Is E14 right for the new one?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "Yes, E14 (SES) is the correct fitting for your Neff oven. Since you have confirmed the old bulb was broken and isolated, please install the new 300°C heat-resistant bulb now. Once fitted, restore power at the cooker switch and test if the light works."
  }
 ],
